  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">DRRS Nigeria 2026 &#x2013; UNICEF WASH Cholera Specialist</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Nigeria continues to face recurrent cholera risk linked to weak water and sanitation services, seasonal flooding, population displacement and humanitarian access challenges. In Maiduguri and Jere, health authorities have reported an ongoing outbreak of acute watery diarrhoea first detected in Shehuri, with affected areas including Gomboru, Old Maiduguri and Bulablin Ali Kotoko, and around 600 admissions reported between 4 and 13 May 2026. Given the likelihood that acute watery diarrhoea may include cholera and the broader national pattern of recurrent outbreaks, UNICEF requires surge WASH capacity to strengthen preparedness, response coordination, infection prevention measures, water quality monitoring, hygiene promotion and rapid support to affected communities. The WASH Cholera Specialist will provide direct programme implementation support to UNICEF, government counterparts and implementing partners to accelerate delivery of cholerapreparedness and response activities in Borno and Yobe States, with a strong focus on outbreak-affected and high-risk communities.</narrative>
  </description>
